The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (skb_shared(skb)) {
    struct sk_buff *nskb = skb_clone(skb, GFP_ATOMIC);
```
```c
can_put_echo_skb() on an unshared SKB
```
```c
can_get_echo_skb() delivers an SKB with a reference count of zero
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because when a CAN driver calls `can_put_echo_skb()` on an unshared SKB and the associated socket is closed before the transmission is completed, `can_get_echo_skb()` attempts to deliver an SKB that has a reference count of zero. This leads to a use-after-free error when the j1939 stack tries to access or clone this invalid SKB.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
struct sk_buff *nskb;
nskb = skb_clone(skb, GFP_ATOMIC);
```
```c
if (unlikely(!nskb)) {
    kfree_skb(skb);
    return NULL;
}
```
Reason:
The fix ensures that `can_get_echo_skb()` always clones the original SKB, regardless of whether it is shared or not. This guarantees that a valid SKB with a proper reference count is delivered, preventing the use-after-free error when the socket is closed. If cloning fails, the original SKB is safely freed, avoiding any invalid access.

